-- title:
--   Toric p-torsion pairs trivially with old p-torsion
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a modulus $M$ with $p \mid M$ but $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing the whole kernel of reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$, and assume $j$ lies in the full modular function field over $\mathbb{Q}$, so that the two-chart integral models are available. Let $\mathfrak{X}$ be an `XHDRModelAtP` datum at $p$ for level $(M,H)$, let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a non-unit of $A$ and with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$, let $\Lambda$ be level data at level $(M/p, H \bmod M/p)$ over $A$, and let $O$ be a `JHNeronObjectAtP` for $(p,M,H,A,\Lambda)$, so $O.G \to \operatorname{Spec} R_p$ carries a relative group law with a Galois- and Hecke-equivariant bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \cdot F(\Gamma_H(M))$ onto its generic-fibre points. Further hypotheses, summarised here, fall into three groups: (i) a relative Picard package — the designation built from $O.G$, $O.g$ and the identity section represents the sub-Picard functor cut out by fibrewise algebraically trivial rigidified line bundles, over $R_p$ (`hD`) and after base change to $\mathbb{Q}$ (`hDQ`), the base-changed curve being separated, with compatibility of the two Poincaré bundles; (ii) an Abel–Jacobi package — morphisms `ajQ`, `kQ`, `ajbar` and a geometric base point `εbar` at the cusp, with `ajQ` carrying the zero section to the zero section and classifying, on points over any field, the line bundle of the divisor of the point twisted by the ideal of the base point, together with the compatibilities `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, the additivity of $O.\mathrm{pts}$ for the relative group law, and the clause `hAJ` realising the class of $(x)-(s)$ as $x$ followed by `ajbar`; (iii) level-$(M/p)$ data — $\Lambda.f$ is smooth and proper with connected fibres and a relative group law, and both points dictionaries $\Lambda.\mathrm{pts}$, $\Lambda.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ are additive. Finally, let $u_H$ be an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra map from the level-$(M/p)$ function field to the level-$M$ one which is integral and finite and satisfies the fundamental identity and the push-forward norm formula, let $i \in \{0,1\}$, and assume $O.\mathrm{degPts}\ i$ computes, on divisor classes, the push-forward of divisors along $u_H$. The conclusion: for every Weil datum $d$ of order $p$ for $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \cdot F(\Gamma_H(M))$ — divisors $D_1, D_2$ with disjoint supports consisting of rational places, and nonzero functions $f_1, f_2$ with $\operatorname{ord}_v f_k = p \, D_k(v)$ for all places $v$ — and all degree-zero divisors $E_1, E_2$ with $E_1 = d.D_1$ and $E_2 = d.D_2$, if the class of $E_1$ lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}\, p$ (the subgroup of $J_H(M)$ generated by the range of $O.\mathrm{toricPoint}\, p$) and if there is a degree-zero divisor $D_w$ at level $(M/p, H \bmod M/p)$ whose class is killed by $p$ with the class of $E_2$ equal to that of the pull-back of $D_w$ along $u_H$, then $d.\mathrm{pairing} = 1$, that is, $\mathrm{evalFun}(f_1, D_2) = \mathrm{evalFun}(f_2, D_1)$.
--
--   This is the orthogonality, for the divisorial Weil pairing of order $p$ on the modular curve of level $(M,H)$, between the toric part of the $p$-torsion and the part pulled back from level $M/p$ along a degeneracy map — one half of Grothendieck's isotropy relations at $\ell = p$ in the level-lowering argument at a prime exactly dividing the level. It is obtained by combining the push–pull adjunction of the divisorial pairing with the vanishing of the degeneracy push-forward on toric $p$-power torsion, and feeds the combined isotropy statement for the full $p$-torsion of $J_H(M)$.
